// specs.memql
//
// forge-namespace specs. Under the spec/shape binding redesign (epic
// #2281) a caller / role predicate is a `spec` bound to an @actor shape
// in its signature (it reads the projected envelope key by bare name and
// evaluates against the auth context). forgeApprover gates the owner-only
// read path (the approval queue) at the query layer, so a non-owner
// cannot list the requests awaiting approval. Submission and "my own
// requests" stay open to every authenticated team member.
//
// The forge personas map onto the cluster's role ladder:
//   owner     -> approver / fast-track
//   admin     -> senior developer (validate + approve)
//   developer -> senior developer (engineering power; validate)
//   writer    -> junior developer (validate)
//   reader    -> non-developer employee (submit-only, mentored)
//
// THE DEVELOPER TIER IS A RANK, NOT A SPEC (memql#5438). It was the
// forgeDeveloper spec -- `actor.role` compared with four slugs -- and three
// gates had to name the same tier: the tools' @allowedRoles, this spec on the
// validation queue, and forgeRequestRoleAllowed on the transition. When they
// disagreed the caller got no error, only an empty queue (memql#4112). The
// tools now declare @requiresRank("writer"), which admits a custom role by its
// rank; a spec cannot read a rank, so it could not have agreed with them. The
// validation queue declares the same @requiresRank("writer"), and the
// transition rule reads the same floor (forgeDeveloperFloor,
// component/memql/forge_request_validation.go) through the same ladder -- one
// floor, so the three cannot disagree.
//
// forgeApprover stays a slug comparison because it is exact: owner is the top
// rung, nothing aliases it, and no role can be created at or above it.

use common.shapes.{ actorEnvelope }

/// Caller is a forge approver (owner) -- holds final approval + fast-track authority.
spec actorEnvelope forgeApprover = actor => actor.role == "owner"
